  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2017-12-22T07:15:51.627Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "With the advent of Playmaker on the horizon, one of the better applications of the scripting system is to have access to an arbitrary external internet server via POST/PUT/GET requests. This would allow persistent data storage per-user for things like storing settings, experience, levels in a game, high score lists, videoSync playlisting, etc etc etc. It would also allow for advanced data processing for interesting applications in worlds. \n\nHowever, it has been brought to my attention that this feature will be disabled due to \"privacy concerns.\"[1] This could very easily be mitigated by warning a user that is joining a world with a popup something like this:\n\n\"This world may transmit information regarding your VRChat identity out to the internet. Do you want to join this world?\n(Yes)    (Yes, and remember my choice)    (No)\"\n\nThis would permit user awareness, allow user safety, and questions regarding the warning could be addressed via a FAQ on the VRChat site and on the Discord. I believe this is the best of both the safety and creativity for worldmakers.\n\nI will note that this warning could also be applied to any world that contains a VideoSync panel, or a WebPanel.\n\n\n[1]I will note that there already exists methods in the game, right now, to gain potentially identifying information (IPs), but this information is extremely low-resolution.",
